This program starts the coding agent in the terminal.

Here is the whole file, as the page lists it:

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt].
// It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(), new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance, Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The `using` lines bring in the framework's namespaces: the web, the three model providers, telemetry, the hosting, the terminal host and the coding demo. `CodingCatalog.Create()` builds the catalogue of identities and packs this program can run. `new HostServices(...)` lends the host the web access, the telemetry and the model providers. `CliHost.RunAsync` builds the agent and runs it in the terminal, taking the `args` as the prompt. The `CliHost` design is in `src/docs/projects/LouisAgent.Cli.md`.

The page's program loads the coding agent's catalogue, so it is not yet your own agent. To make one, you write your own catalogue, with your identities and packs. The page doesn't show that code, and I didn't find it in the docs.

The settings are read from the demo's `config` folder. Put your real key in `.env.secrets`, in place of the placeholder:

```bash
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-key-here
LLM_PROFILE=ollama
```

`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` goes in `.env.secrets`. `LLM_PROFILE=ollama` picks a local model; leave it out to use Anthropic. Then run `dotnet run` from `demos/louis-agent/hosts/LouisAgent.CodingCli`. The steps are in `src/docs/guides/get-started.md`.
